Sval: a Peek is neither the frontmost window nor a restored one

A Peek is a full browser window that holds one page from another
app, in no workspace (SvalPeek). BrowserWindowTracker.getTopWindow
answers "the window to open this in" for Firefox's callers, a link from
another app with the Peek turned off among them, and it skips a
popup for the same reason unless asked. No chrome API takes a window out of
its answer: a popup's toolbar.visible, the only other way, makes the
address bar read-only and the titlebar native, and the Peek carries
the main window's address bar and titlebar.

For the same reason SessionStore's restore of the last closed window, which
macOS runs for the next window after every one is closed, skips a Peek
as it skips a popup: the Peek would take that window's tabs.

Firefox's quick actions and search terms persistence ask getTopWindow for
the window whose address bar is in use, which a Peek's can be: they
take it with allowPopups, or with only a Peek open they get null and
throw, and the throw drops every quick action from the address bar.

openPreferences opens Settings in the most recent browser window, a Peek
among them, whose new tab opens another Peek, or an agent's
window, whose overlay holds the user's input and whose tabs are the agent's.
It asks SvalPeek.settingsWindow for the user's own window instead.

